You’re raising a really important limitation, and I think your framing around probability distributions is a big driver.

AI models are optimized to predict the most likely next step based on patterns they’ve seen before, not to truly “explore” a problem space. This can be because of focal loss, due to statistically common parings, not just because they are best fit. There can be the illusion of confidence while missing very context-specific approaches.

I also agree with your point on focal loss being overused. Its effectiveness really depends on the structure of the data and the problem itself. Sometimes simpler strategies, like resampling, adjusting class weights, or even redefining the objective, end up performing better. It highlights the gap between what’s usually recommended and what’s actually best.

One thing I’d add is that this tendency can be managed depending on how the question is framed. If you push AI to justify its choices, compare alternatives, or identify when a solution might fail, it tends to move away from that “default path” thinking. Without that, it’s essentially pattern-matching its way through the problem.

So I don’t think it’s just a limitation, it’s more of a bias toward familiarity. And like any bias, it becomes much more noticeable in open-ended exploration than in structured tasks like coding.


